Chapter 2

Mathematical Preliminaries

We work in a probability space (\Omega,\mathcal{F},\mathbb{P}). All variables are finite‑dimensional and admit densities with respect to a suitable base measure. Capital letters denote random variables; lower case denotes realisations. The mutual information between X and Y is

I(X;Y)=D_{\mathrm{KL}}\big(p(x,y)\,\|\,p(x)p(y)\big),

with conditional mutual information I(X;Y|Z)=\mathbb{E}_Z D_{\mathrm{KL}}(p(x,y|z)\,\|\,p(x|z)p(y|z)). The entropy of a discrete variable is H(X); for continuous variables differential entropy is used with care.

2.1 Observational and Causal Process

The agent receives a sensory stream O_1,O_2,\dots generated by a stationary, ergodic hidden Markov process with latent causal state S_t. The causal state (Shalizi & Crutchfield 2001) is the equivalence class of all past observation histories that yield the same conditional distribution over future observations:

s_t = \epsilon(o_{\le t}) \iff p(o_{>t}\,|\,o_{\le t}) = p(o_{>t}\,|\,s_t).

The mapping to causal states is the unique minimal sufficient statistic for the future, and it satisfies I(S_t;O_{>t}) = I(O_{\le t};O_{>t}).

2.2 Rate‑Distortion and the Information Bottleneck

A lossy compression of the past into a representation Z is described by a stochastic encoder q(z|o_{\le t}). The information rate is I(Z;O_{\le t}). Given a distortion function d(o_{\le t}, z) measuring the cost of representing the past by z, the rate‑distortion function R(D) gives the minimal rate such that average distortion does not exceed D.

The information bottleneck (Tishby et al. 2000) chooses a different distortion: the loss of information about a relevance variable Y. For time‑series, the natural relevance is the future O_{>t}. The Predictive Information Bottleneck (PIB; Creutzig & Sprekeler 2008; Still 2014) maximizes

\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{PIB}} = I(Z;O_{>t}) - \beta I(Z;O_{\le t}),

where \beta^{-1} controls the trade‑off between compression and predictive fidelity. When \beta \to 0, compression is maximal; when \beta \to \infty, the bottleneck preserves all predictive information.

2.3 Free Energy as a Variational Bound

Although we do not assume the free‑energy principle as a primitive, we will use it later as an analytically convenient objective. For a generative model p(o,s) and an approximate posterior q(s),

F(o;q) = \mathbb{E}_{q(s)}[\ln q(s) - \ln p(o,s)] \ge -\ln p(o).

Minimizing F improves the model’s evidence bound and, under active inference, drives adaptive behaviour.

