Chapter 3

The Predictive Sufficiency Lemma

3.1 Statement

Lemma 3.1 (Predictive Sufficiency under Finite Capacity).
An agent is equipped with an internal state Z_t that encodes the sensory past O_{\le t}. The agent must minimise a cumulative cost

J = \lim_{T\to\infty} \frac{1}{T} \sum_{\tau=1}^{T} \mathbb{E}\!\left[-\ln p_{\mathrm{agent}}(o_{>\tau}\,|\,z_\tau) \right] + \lambda \, \mathrm{Memory}(Z_t),

where \mathrm{Memory}(Z_t) is the minimal number of bits required to store Z_t on average, i.e., I(Z_t;O_{\le t}), and \lambda > 0 is the relative cost of memory. Then the optimal encoder q^*(z|o_{\le t}) maximises the predictive information I(Z;O_{>t}) subject to I(Z;O_{\le t}) \le C for a capacity C determined by \lambda. Equivalently, q^* is a solution to the Predictive Information Bottleneck problem.

3.2 Proof

Step 1: Cost function formulation.
The agent’s immediate objective is to predict the future. We measure prediction error by the log‑loss -\ln p_{\mathrm{agent}}(o_{>t}|z_t) under the agent’s own model. This is a standard distortion measure in compression; it is the only local, proper scoring rule. The expected instantaneous distortion is H(O_{>t}|Z_t) (the cross‑entropy between the true conditional distribution and the agent’s model), plus a calibration term that vanishes if the agent’s model matches the truth. For the optimal agent that has learned the correct conditional distribution of the future given z_t, the minimal achievable distortion is exactly the conditional entropy H(O_{>t}|Z_t). Hence, without loss of generality, we can take the distortion to be H(O_{>t}|Z_t).

The memory cost of maintaining Z_t is given by the rate I(Z_t;O_{\le t}). This is the minimal number of bits (in the Shannon sense) required to encode the past. Any physical realisation of the agent must satisfy Landauer’s principle: maintaining and updating the internal state dissipates energy proportional to this mutual information. Therefore the total expected cost per time step is

\mathcal{C} = H(O_{>t}|Z) + \lambda I(Z;O_{\le t}).

Because the process is stationary, the time index is dropped.

Step 2: Reduction to the Information Bottleneck.
Using the identity I(Z;O_{>t}) = H(O_{>t}) - H(O_{>t}|Z), we rewrite the cost:

\mathcal{C} = H(O_{>t}) - I(Z;O_{>t}) + \lambda I(Z;O_{\le t}).

Since H(O_{>t}) is a constant of the environment, minimising \mathcal{C} is equivalent to maximising

I(Z;O_{>t}) - \lambda I(Z;O_{\le t}).

Thus the optimal encoder for a given \lambda solves the PIB problem with \beta = 1/\lambda. The monotonic relationship between \lambda and the achieved rate I(Z;O_{\le t}) defines an effective capacity C(\lambda). For any desired capacity C, there exists a \lambda such that the optimal encoder satisfies I(Z;O_{\le t}) \le C. Hence the lemma is proved.

Step 3: Variational free energy as an implementation.
The log‑loss objective -\ln p(o_{>t}|z) can be bounded above by a variational free energy:

-\ln p(o_{>t}|z) \le \mathbb{E}_{q(s|z)}[\ln q(s|z) - \ln p(o_{>t}, s|z)].

When the agent uses this bound and additionally incorporates a prior over representations, the PIB Lagrangian emerges as the objective. Thus the free energy principle is derived as a biologically plausible approximation to the predictive‑compression imperative, not the other way around.

