Chapter 4

The Predictive Compression Theorem

4.1 Statement

Theorem 4.1 (Predictive Compression Drives Abstraction).
Let the environment be generated by a stationary hidden Markov process with causal state S_t. Suppose an agent with capacity C implements an optimal encoder according to Lemma 3.1. Then as C \to \log_2 |\mathcal{S}| from above, the representation Z converges in information to the causal state partition: Z becomes a deterministic function of S_t, and I(Z;O_{>t}) \to I(S_t;O_{>t}). For any finite C, the optimal representation coarse‑grains the causal states, retaining only those distinctions that carry the most predictive value given the capacity bound. Consequently, the internal model necessarily abstracts the causal skeleton of the environment from raw sensory data.

4.2 Proof

Step 1: Optimal representation and the Markov chain.
From Lemma 3.1, the optimal Z maximises I(Z;O_{>t}) given a rate constraint. The data processing inequality for the Markov chain O_{\le t} \to S_t \to O_{>t} (where S_t is a sufficient statistic) implies

I(Z;O_{>t}) \le I(S_t;O_{>t}) = I(O_{\le t};O_{>t}).

Equality is achievable if and only if Z is a sufficient statistic for the future, i.e., if p(o_{>t}|z) = p(o_{>t}|s_t) whenever z and s_t correspond to the same observation history. The minimal such sufficient statistic is precisely the causal state \epsilon(o_{\le t}) (Shalizi & Crutchfield 2001). Therefore, when the capacity C is at least H(S_t), the optimal encoder can set Z = S_t and achieve the upper bound.

Step 2: Finite capacity and coarse‑graining.
When C < H(S_t), the encoder cannot maintain a distinct representation for every causal state. The PIB problem then demands a compression of the causal states into a smaller alphabet. Let the optimal encoder be described by a stochastic matrix q(z|s). The objective becomes

\max_{q(z|s)} I(Z;O_{>t}) \quad \text{s.t.} \quad I(Z;S) \le C,

where we used the Markov chain Z \leftarrow S \leftarrow O_{\le t}. This is exactly a rate‑distortion problem with distortion d(s,z) = D_{\mathrm{KL}}\big(p(o_{>t}|s) \,\|\, p(o_{>t}|z)\big) (the loss in predictive information when merging states). The optimal solution maps multiple causal states to the same representation z, grouping those with similar predictive distributions. The resulting clusters are equivalence classes that respect predictive similarity; they form a coarse‑grained causal model. This is the formal mechanism of abstraction.

Step 3: Connection to conceptual content.
The equivalence classes are not yet semantic concepts like “prey” or “predator”; they are purely formal, defined by predictive equivalence. However, in an agent that acts, these classes become the natural basis for decision‑making. A cluster that predicts sharp sensory transients and requires rapid evasion can, through the agent’s sensorimotor contingencies, acquire the functional role of a “predator”. The mathematics guarantees that the skeleton of such concepts—the causal invariants—is extracted automatically. The intentional content must be supplied by the agent’s embodiment and goals, a further step this thesis does not formalise but for which it provides the necessary foundation.

