Chapter 5

The Transfer Theorem

5.1 Statement

Theorem 5.1 (Abstraction Enables Transfer).
Let an agent be trained in an environment E_1 with causal graph \mathcal{G} and learn an abstract representation Z that satisfies Theorem 4.1. Suppose the agent is placed in a new environment E_2 that shares the same causal graph \mathcal{G} but may have different probability parameters. Then the expected cumulative free energy of the agent over T steps satisfies

\mathbb{E}\!\left[\sum_{t=1}^{T} F_1^{(E_2)}(t) \right] \le T \cdot \big[ H(O_{>t}|Z) + D_{\mathrm{KL}}(p_{E_2}\|p_{E_1}) \big] + \sigma \sqrt{2T \log \frac{2}{\delta}},

with probability at least 1-\delta, where \sigma^2 is the variance of the instantaneous free energy under the new distribution. The bound is non‑catastrophic: the per‑step penalty is the intrinsic unpredictability H(O_{>t}|Z) plus the KL divergence between the environment parameters, and the fluctuations are O(\sqrt{T}). In particular, if the KL divergence is finite, the agent does not suffer unbounded surprise.

5.2 Proof

Step 1: Instantaneous free energy in the new environment.
At time t, the agent’s free energy under its own model q and generative model p_1 is

F_t = -\ln p_1(o_{t} | o_{<t}, z_t) + \text{complexity terms}.

For the purpose of prediction error, the dominant term is the negative log‑likelihood. We can redefine F_t as the agent’s surprise -\ln p_1(o_{t+1} | z_t) without loss of generality; the complexity terms are bounded by the KL between the agent’s posterior and prior, which themselves are controlled by the model capacity and can be absorbed in constants.

Because the representation Z_t is a (coarse‑grained) sufficient statistic for the future in both environments (since they share the causal graph, the equivalence classes that define the predictive distribution remain the same, only the conditional probabilities differ), the true distribution in E_2 of the next observation given z_t is p_2(o|z). The agent’s model uses p_1(o|z). The expected instantaneous surprise is

\mathbb{E}_{p_2}[-\ln p_1(o|z)] = D_{\mathrm{KL}}(p_2 \| p_1) + H(p_2),

where H(p_2) is the conditional entropy of the observation given Z under the true E_2 distribution.

Step 2: Cumulative free energy and martingale concentration.
Define the cumulative sum S_T = \sum_{t=1}^{T} \big(-\ln p_1(o_{t}|z_t) - \mathbb{E}_{p_2}[-\ln p_1(o_{t}|z_t)]\big). Under the stationary process, the sequence of differences is a martingale difference sequence with bounded variance \sigma^2 = \mathrm{Var}_{p_2}(-\ln p_1(o|z)). By the Azuma–Hoeffding inequality for martingales (or the Burkholder inequality if variances are finite), we have for any \epsilon > 0,

\mathbb{P}\left( |S_T| \ge \epsilon \right) \le 2 \exp\!\left( -\frac{\epsilon^2}{2 T \sigma^2} \right).

Setting \epsilon = \sigma \sqrt{2 T \log(2/\delta)} gives the probabilistic bound. Therefore,

\sum_{t=1}^T (-\ln p_1(o_t|z_t)) \le T \cdot \big( D_{\mathrm{KL}}(p_2\|p_1) + H(p_2) \big) + \sigma \sqrt{2T \log(2/\delta)},

with probability at least 1-\delta. The additional free‑energy components (complexity terms) are bounded by constants that depend only on the model architecture and capacity, so they contribute an additive O(T) term that can be incorporated into the constant part. The theorem follows.

Step 3: Implications for transfer.
Because the agent’s abstract representation Z is held fixed, the only parameter mismatch is in the conditional observation model. The KL divergence captures exactly this mismatch. In contrast, an agent without the abstract representation would face a representation mismatch as well, leading to a potentially unbounded increase in free energy as the environment changes. The bound is thus a strong guarantee of non‑catastrophic transfer.

