Chapter 6

Recursive Self‑Modelling and the Efficiency Selection Theorem

6.1 Recursive Architecture

We now introduce a second‑order observer that monitors the performance of the first‑order agent. The object‑level agent (Level 1) operates with an internal representation Z_t^{(1)} and maintains an inference algorithm M \in \mathcal{M}, where \mathcal{M} is a finite set of candidate algorithms (e.g., learning rates, model orders, or optimisation routines). The meta‑level (Level 2) receives as observation the cumulative free energy (or prediction error) of Level 1 over a recent window:

o^{(2)}_t = \frac{1}{\Delta} \sum_{\tau = t-\Delta+1}^{t} F_1(\tau; M).

Level 2 updates its belief over \mathcal{M} using the replicator dynamics

p_{t+1}(M_i) = \frac{p_t(M_i) \exp\!\big(-\eta_t \, o^{(2)}_t(M_i)\big)}{\sum_j p_t(M_j) \exp\!\big(-\eta_t \, o^{(2)}_t(M_j)\big)},

where \eta_t is a decreasing learning rate satisfying \sum \eta_t = \infty and \sum \eta_t^2 < \infty. This is a stochastic approximation of the gradient flow on the meta‑free energy

\mathcal{F}(p) = \sum_i p_i \bar{F}_i + \frac{1}{\eta} H(p),

with \bar{F}_i = \mathbb{E}[F_1 \mid M_i].

6.2 Statement

Theorem 6.1 (Recursive Efficiency Selection).
Assume:

1. The environment is stationary and ergodic.
2. For each M_i \in \mathcal{M}, the resulting object‑level free energy process \{F_1(t; M_i)\}_{t\ge 1} is stationary and ergodic with finite mean \bar{F}_i and finite variance.
3. The means \bar{F}_i are distinct: \bar{F}_i \neq \bar{F}_j for i \neq j.
4. The meta‑level samples each operator infinitely often (e.g., via a softmax policy that includes an exploration term decaying to zero, or by using the above replicator update with full feedback).

Then the belief vector p_t converges almost surely to a delta distribution on the operator M^* = \arg\min_i \bar{F}_i.

Moreover, because the free energy \bar{F}_i is minimised by the algorithm that best approximates the PIB optimum (Lemma 3.1), the selected operator implements an inference procedure that optimally compresses for the future, thereby closing the loop from finite memory to self‑improving intelligence.

6.3 Proof

The dynamics (6.1) are exactly a multi‑armed bandit with softmax action selection and full information (the meta‑level observes the costs of all operators). Under the stated conditions, this is a special case of the generalised stochastic approximation for games (Börgers & Sarin 1997; Ljung 1977). We sketch the core argument.

Define the empirical average cost up to time t as \hat{F}_i(t) = \frac{1}{t} \sum_{\tau=1}^t o^{(2)}_\tau(M_i). The update can be rewritten as a gradient step on the entropy‑regularised objective. With decreasing \eta_t, the noise in the cost estimates averages out, and the dynamics track the mean‑field ODE

\dot{p}_i = -p_i \big( \bar{F}_i - \sum_j p_j \bar{F}_j \big) + \frac{1}{\eta_t} (\text{entropy gradient}).

As t \to \infty, the entropic term vanishes because \eta_t \to 0. The deterministic dynamics then converge to the simplex face spanned by the minimisers of \bar{F}_i. Since the minima are unique (distinct means), the only stable equilibrium is p^* = e_{i^*}, where i^* is the index of the strictly smallest \bar{F}. Standard stochastic approximation theory guarantees that the stochastic algorithm converges almost surely to this equilibrium under the Robbins‑Monro conditions.

Identifiability condition in detail.
The condition \bar{F}_i \neq \bar{F}_j ensures that the objective landscape has no flat directions that would prevent convergence to a single operator. In practice, if two operators yield exactly the same average free energy, the dynamics converge to a mixture over them, which is perfectly acceptable and does not compromise the selection pressure towards efficiency. The theorem’s claim of convergence to a single operator can be relaxed to convergence to the set of optimisers without affecting the conclusion.

On the exploration requirement.
To guarantee that each operator is sampled infinitely often, we may either add a vanishing exploration term (e.g., \epsilon_t-greedy) to the replicator update, or rely on the softmax probabilities themselves, which remain positive for finite times. Since \eta_t \to 0, the stochastic updates continue to probe all operators with a frequency that vanishes slowly enough to satisfy the persistent excitation condition. The theorem holds under these standard extensions.

