Chapter 8

Falsifiability and Experimental Predictions

A theory of intelligence must be falsifiable. The framework presented yields several concrete predictions that can be tested in artificial and biological systems.

Prediction 1 – Adaptive compression in volatile environments.
An agent with a fixed memory budget, placed in a non‑stationary world where the causal structure changes, should quickly reorganise its internal representation to capture the new predictive invariants. The cumulative free energy should recover rapidly after a regime shift. An agent without the predictive compression objective (e.g., a uniform compressor) will either exhaust its memory or fail to adapt. This can be tested in grid‑world simulations.

Prediction 2 – Neural signature of meta‑level selection.
If the meta‑level dynamics of REST are implemented by neuromodulatory systems (such as dopamine), then the temporal derivative of dopaminergic signals should correlate with the gradient of the meta‑free energy, i.e., with the difference between expected and recently observed cumulative performance. Existing electrophysiological data may already contain this signature.

Prediction 3 – Emergence of causal abstractions in neural networks.
Training a recurrent neural network with an explicit information‑bottleneck objective (e.g., penalising I(Z; X_{\mathrm{past}}) while minimising prediction error) should cause the hidden representation to align with the causal states of the environment. The degree of alignment, measured by mutual information with the true latent causal factors, should increase as the capacity is tightened, up to the point where the bottleneck becomes too severe. This can be tested on synthetic HMMs and more complex video prediction tasks.

Failure to observe any of these phenomena would necessitate revision or rejection of the theory.

