Appendix B

Rate-Distortion Theory and the Predictive Information Bottleneck

The classical rate-distortion problem (Cover & Thomas, 2006) seeks a compressor q(z|x) that minimises the rate I(Z;X) while keeping average distortion \mathbb{E}[d(X,Z)] \le D. The optimal trade-off defines the rate-distortion function R(D). When the distortion is the log-loss d(x,z)= -\ln p(x|z) for a given conditional probability model, the problem becomes equivalent to the information bottleneck (Tishby et al., 2000) if one identifies the relevance variable Y with X itself. However, the information bottleneck generalises this by keeping a different variable Y relevant: the objective is to maximise I(Z;Y) - \beta I(Z;X). For time series, the natural choice is X = X_{\le t} (the past) and Y = X_{>t} (the future). This is the Predictive Information Bottleneck (PIB) (Creutzig & Sprekeler, 2008; Still, 2014).

The PIB Lagrangian is

\mathcal{L}[q(z|x)] = I(Z;X_{>t}) - \beta I(Z;X_{\le t}),

with \beta \ge 0. The optimal encoder satisfies the self-consistent equations (derived from a generalised Blahut-Arimoto algorithm):

q(z|x) = \frac{p(z)}{Z(x,\beta)} \exp\!\Big( -\beta \, D_{\mathrm{KL}}\big(p(x_{>t}|x) \,\big\|\, p(x_{>t}|z) \big) \Big),

where p(z) = \sum_x p(x) q(z|x), and Z(x,\beta) is a normalisation. The implied conditional distribution over the future given z is

p(x_{>t}|z) = \frac{1}{p(z)} \sum_x p(x) q(z|x) p(x_{>t}|x).

The algorithm converges to a deterministic mapping as \beta \to \infty, and to a completely uninformative clustering as \beta \to 0. For finite \beta, the representation retains only those details of the past that help predict the future, exactly implementing the predictive sufficiency idea.

