Appendix C

Proof of Lemma 3.1 (Predictive Sufficiency Lemma)

We restate the lemma precisely:

Lemma 3.1. Let an agent possess a memory capacity C (bits) that bounds the mutual information I(Z;O_{\le t}) \le C. The agent selects an internal representation Z and a predictive model p(o_{>t}|z) to minimise the long-run expected cost

\mathcal{C} = \lim_{T\to\infty} \frac{1}{T} \sum_{\tau=1}^T \Big[ \mathbb{E}\big[-\ln p(o_{>\tau}|z_\tau)\big] + \lambda \, I(Z_\tau; O_{\le \tau}) \Big],

where \lambda > 0 is the relative price of memory. Then the optimal encoder q^*(z|o_{\le t}) solves the PIB problem with \beta = 1/\lambda, i.e., it maximises I(Z;O_{>t}) - \beta I(Z;O_{\le t}).

Proof. Under stationarity and ergodicity, the time average equals the ensemble average, so the cost per step is

\mathcal{C} = \mathbb{E}\big[-\ln p(o_{>t}|z)\big] + \lambda I(Z;O_{\le t}),

where the expectation is over the joint distribution of (O_{\le t}, Z, O_{>t}) induced by the environment and the encoder. For any encoder and any predictive model p(o_{>t}|z), the expected log-loss satisfies

\mathbb{E}\big[-\ln p(o_{>t}|z)\big] \ge H(O_{>t}|Z),

with equality if and only if p(o_{>t}|z) equals the true conditional distribution p^*(o_{>t}|z). Since the agent can learn the true conditional from experience (and we are considering the optimal performance limit), we may replace the model by the true distribution, and the distortion becomes exactly H(O_{>t}|Z). Hence the cost reduces to

\mathcal{C} = H(O_{>t}|Z) + \lambda I(Z;O_{\le t}).

Using the identity I(Z;O_{>t}) = H(O_{>t}) - H(O_{>t}|Z), we have

\mathcal{C} = H(O_{>t}) - I(Z;O_{>t}) + \lambda I(Z;O_{\le t}).

Since H(O_{>t}) is a constant of the environment, minimising \mathcal{C} is equivalent to maximising

I(Z;O_{>t}) - \lambda I(Z;O_{\le t}).

Setting \beta = 1/\lambda yields the PIB objective. The capacity constraint I(Z;O_{\le t}) \le C is automatically satisfied by appropriate choice of \lambda because there is a monotonic relationship between \lambda and the achieved rate R = I(Z;O_{\le t}): as \lambda increases, the effective rate decreases. Thus, for any desired capacity C, there exists a \lambda such that the optimal encoder under the unrestricted cost (C.1) yields I(Z;O_{\le t}) \le C. The lemma follows.

Note on memory cost interpretation. The term \lambda I(Z;O_{\le t}) can be rigorously justified as the minimal thermodynamic cost of storing the representation Z at the given precision, via Landauer’s principle (Appendix I). Hence the cost function is physically well-grounded, not an ad hoc choice.

