Appendix D

Computational Mechanics and Causal States

We follow Shalizi & Crutchfield (2001). For a stationary process \{X_t\}, define the equivalence relation

x_{\le t} \sim x'_{\le t} \iff p(X_{>t}\,|\,X_{\le t}=x_{\le t}) = p(X_{>t}\,|\,X_{\le t}=x'_{\le t}) \quad \text{a.e.}

The equivalence classes are the causal states S_t = \epsilon(X_{\le t}). The causal state process \{S_t\} is a deterministic hidden Markov model (a unifilar HMM) that is the unique minimal sufficient statistic for predicting the future. The complexity of the process is measured by the statistical complexity C_\mu = H[S], and the predictive information is I(S;X_{>t}) = I(X_{\le t};X_{>t}).

The \epsilon-machine is the automaton whose states are the causal states and whose transitions and emission probabilities are determined by the process. The \epsilon-machine is optimal in the sense that no alternative HMM can have smaller state entropy while retaining the same predictive ability.

The crucial connection to our work: the solution of the PIB at a given compression rate R = I(Z;X_{\le t}) can be seen as a coarse-grained causal state model. When R \ge C_\mu, the optimal Z can be exactly the causal state mapping (or a bijection thereof). When R < C_\mu, the PIB merges causal states with similar predictive distributions, preserving as much predictive information as possible. The resulting partition is a coarse-grained causal model that respects predictive equivalence.

