Appendix E

Proof of Theorem 4.1 (Predictive Compression Theorem)

Theorem 4.1. Under the assumptions of Lemma 3.1, as the capacity constraint C \to \log |\mathcal{S}| (the entropy of the causal state), the optimal representation Z converges in information to the causal state mapping. For any finite C, the representation coarse-grains the causal states into clusters with minimal predictive distortion.

Proof. From Lemma 3.1, the optimal encoder solves \max I(Z;O_{>t}) - \beta I(Z;O_{\le t}) with a Lagrange multiplier \beta corresponding to the capacity C. The data processing inequality applied to the Markov chain O_{\le t} \to S \to O_{>t} gives

I(Z;O_{>t}) \le I(S;O_{>t}) = I(O_{\le t}; O_{>t}).

Equality can be achieved if and only if Z is a sufficient statistic for the future. The minimal sufficient statistic is the causal state map \epsilon. Hence when the capacity C \ge H(S), the optimal encoder can set Z = \epsilon(O_{\le t}) (or any bijection), achieving I(Z;O_{>t}) = I(S;O_{>t}) and I(Z;O_{\le t}) = H(S) = C_\mu. The objective is then maximised, and any further increase in capacity would not improve predictive information.

When C < H(S), the rate constraint I(Z;O_{\le t}) \le C forces Z to compress the causal states. The PIB problem reduces to maximising I(Z;O_{>t}) subject to I(Z;S) \le C because Z is a function of S (any optimal encoder can be taken as a deterministic function of the causal state without loss of optimality, since stochasticity only adds noise). The distortion now is the loss in predictive information when merging states: if s_i and s_j are mapped to the same z, the resulting conditional distribution over futures becomes a mixture of their individual distributions, increasing the entropy H(O_{>t}|z). The optimal partition of causal states is therefore the one that minimises the average increase in conditional entropy subject to the rate constraint, which is exactly the solution of the rate-distortion problem with distortion measure d(s,z) = D_{\mathrm{KL}}\big(p(O_{>t}|s) \,\|\, p(O_{>t}|z)\big). This is solved by the Information Bottleneck algorithm (Tishby et al., 2000) with the causal state as the “input” and the future as the “relevance”. The resulting clusters group causal states whose predictive distributions are most similar. This formalises abstraction as predictive coarse-graining. ∎

