Appendix G

Proof of Theorem 6.1 (Recursive Efficiency Selection Theorem)

Theorem 6.1. Under the assumptions (stationarity, ergodicity, finite variance, distinct mean free energies \bar{F}_i, and infinite exploration of each operator), the belief vector p_t updated via replicator dynamics converges almost surely to a vertex corresponding to the operator with the smallest expected free energy.

Proof. The update is

p_{t+1}(i) = \frac{p_t(i) \exp\big(-\eta_t \hat{F}_i(t)\big)}{\sum_j p_t(j) \exp\big(-\eta_t \hat{F}_j(t)\big)},

where \hat{F}_i(t) is the empirical mean of the observed free energy of operator i up to time t (or a recent window). With full information, the meta-level observes the cost of all operators each step (e.g., by running them in parallel or in a simulated forward model). Then \hat{F}_i(t) is the sample mean of the costs incurred when operator i was chosen, or if all are observed simultaneously, we can treat the update as using the true mean with noise. The standard approach is to consider the ODE associated with the algorithm. Let \bar{F}_i be the true stationary mean. The stochastic process can be shown to track the deterministic replicator dynamics

\dot{p}_i = -p_i (\bar{F}_i - \sum_j p_j \bar{F}_j).

Define the entropy function H(p)=-\sum_i p_i \ln p_i. The Lyapunov function \mathcal{V}(p) = \sum_i p_i \bar{F}_i - \frac{1}{\eta} H(p) (with \eta a constant before decaying) decreases along the trajectories. Since \eta_t \to 0 and the cost estimates become more accurate, the system converges to the set of minima of \sum_i p_i \bar{F}_i, which is the convex hull of the operators with minimal \bar{F}_i. When these minima are unique (distinct means), the only stable equilibrium is the pure strategy e_{i^*} with i^* = \arg\min_i \bar{F}_i. Standard stochastic approximation results (Benveniste, Métivier, & Priouret, 1990; Borkar, 2008) guarantee almost sure convergence of the discrete-time stochastic recursion to the stable fixed points of the ODE, provided the step sizes satisfy \sum \eta_t = \infty, \sum \eta_t^2 < \infty and the noise is a martingale difference with bounded variance. The condition of infinite exploration can be satisfied by adding a vanishing exploration term \epsilon_t \to 0 (e.g., \epsilon_t = 1/t^\alpha) to the softmax, which does not alter the asymptotic convergence. Therefore p_t \to e_{i^*} a.s. ∎

The proof also requires the identifiability condition: \bar{F}_i are distinct. If there is a set of optimal operators with identical mean, the dynamics converge to the simplex spanned by them, still selecting the optimal subset. This is sufficient for the selection pressure towards efficiency.

