Appendix H

Worked Example: Two-State Hidden Markov Model with Full Computations

We present an analytically tractable example that illustrates the entire theory.

Environment. Let the hidden state S_t \in \{0,1\} evolve with transition probabilities P(S_{t+1}=1|S_t=0)=\alpha=0.1, P(S_{t+1}=0|S_t=1)=\beta=0.1. Observations O_t \in \{0,1\} have emission probabilities \phi_0 = P(O=1|S=0)=0.2, \phi_1 = P(O=1|S=1)=0.8. The process is stationary with equilibrium belief state b = P(S=1) determined by the Markov chain.

Causal states. Because the true state is hidden, the causal state is the belief b_t = P(S_t=1 \mid o_{\le t}). The belief is updated via Bayes rule and takes values in the interval [b_{\min},b_{\max}]. For these parameters, the set of possible beliefs is finite (since it is a deterministic function of a finite-state machine). The statistical complexity C_\mu = H(B) can be computed from the stationary distribution over beliefs. It turns out C_\mu \approx 0.97 bits.

Predictive information bottleneck solution. We impose a capacity of C = 1 bit and compute the PIB optimal mapping. The Blahut-Arimoto algorithm adapted to the bottleneck yields a partition of the belief interval into two regions: “low” (belief < \theta) and “high” (belief \ge \theta), with \theta \approx 0.5. The resulting representation Z \in \{L,H\} captures I(Z;O_{>t}) \approx 0.42 bits, close to the full predictive information I(B;O_{>t}) \approx 0.47 bits. The loss is small because the two clusters have very distinct predictive distributions: the “low” cluster predicts mostly O=0, and the “high” cluster predicts mostly O=1.

Transfer. Now consider E_2 with the same transition probabilities but reversed emissions: \phi'_0 = 0.8, \phi'_1 = 0.2. The causal graph (the belief update dynamics) is unchanged, so the partition L/H remains valid. The conditional distributions, however, swap: in the “low” cluster, the future observation is now mostly 1, and in the “high” cluster mostly 0. The KL divergence between the new and old predictive distributions for each cluster is D_{\mathrm{KL}}(p'_L \| p_L) \approx 0.83 nats (for each step). The average divergence \bar{D}_{\mathrm{KL}} \approx 0.83. The residual entropy H_2 per step is about 0.50 nats. Thus the bound for T=1000 steps is 1000 \times (0.83+0.50) + \sigma \sqrt{2000 \log(2/0.05)} \approx 1330 + 2.24 \sigma. The empirical standard deviation of the log-loss in E_2 is \sigma \approx 0.9, giving a total bound of \approx 1332, compared to a purely random initial model which would yield a far larger cumulative surprise.

Recursive selection. Three inference algorithms are compared: M1 (exact Bayesian filter, full belief representation, memory usage ≈ C_\mu \approx 0.97 bits), M2 (the PIB 1-bit model), M3 (a fixed classifier that ignores recent observations). The free energy (log-loss) under each is simulated. With a memory cost multiplier \lambda = 0.1, the effective cost per step (log-loss + \lambda \times memory) is: M1: 0.47 + 0.097 = 0.567, M2: 0.50 + 0.1 = 0.60, M3: 0.69 + 0.0 = 0.69. The replicator dynamics quickly concentrate on M1, but under a tighter capacity budget (larger \lambda), M2 becomes optimal. The example demonstrates that the optimal inference machinery is selected by the meta-level dynamics, and that this machinery implements the predictive compression optimum.

