Appendix I

Landauer’s Principle and the Thermodynamic Cost of Memory

Landauer (1961) showed that erasing one bit of information in a memory at temperature T dissipates at least k_B T \ln 2 of energy into the environment. In a steady-state agent, maintaining a memory state with entropy H(Z) and mutual information I(Z;O_{\le t}) about the past requires continual energy expenditure to counteract thermal degradation and to update the state. The minimal energy cost per unit time for a physical memory that stores a representation with rate R = I(Z;O_{\le t}) is proportional to R (Still et al., 2012). Hence the cost term \lambda I(Z;O_{\le t}) in our objective function is not merely a mathematical convenience but a physically necessary penalty for limited resources. This establishes a direct link to thermodynamics and further justifies the predictive compression imperative as emerging from physical law.

---

Appendix J

Relationship between the Free Energy Principle and Predictive Compression

We demonstrate that the variational free energy of active inference is a special case of the predictive compression cost when the agent uses a generative model with latent states and a recognition density. Starting from the PIB objective \mathcal{C}_{\mathrm{PIB}} = H(O_{>t}|Z) + \lambda I(Z;O_{\le t}), we can upper-bound the first term by any variational bound based on a model p(o_{>t}, s|z) and an approximate posterior q(s|o_{\le t},z):

H(O_{>t}|Z) \le \mathbb{E}_{p^*(o_{>t}, z)}\big[-\ln p(o_{>t}|z)\big] \le \mathbb{E}_{q(s|z)}\big[ \ln q(s|z) - \ln p(o_{>t}, s|z) \big] = F_{\mathrm{var}}.

If we also introduce a prior over the representation p(z) and use the standard variational identity, the full free energy becomes

\mathcal{F} = \mathbb{E}_{q(z, s|o)}[\ln q(z, s|o) - \ln p(o, z, s)],

which is precisely the free energy functional used in FEP. Minimising this \mathcal{F} simultaneously encourages compression (through the KL between q(z) and the prior) and predictive accuracy (through the expected log-likelihood). Thus the free energy principle can be seen as a variational implementation of the predictive compression imperative when the agent uses a hierarchical generative model. The derivation inverts the usual logic: instead of starting with FEP and deriving behaviour, we start from the unavoidable physical constraints of memory and predictive pressure, and derive FEP as a computational tool. This resolves the circularity noted in earlier drafts: the FEP is no longer the axiomatic primitive.

